WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.198 --> 00:00:10.559
“I desired liberty; for liberty I gasped;
for liberty I uttered a prayer; it seemed

00:00:10.559 --> 00:00:14.040
scattered on the wind then faintly blowing.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:20.030
I abandoned it, and framed a humbler supplication;
for change, stimulus…”

00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:22.540
Me during quarantine.

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:27.460
Romping about the moors with your star-crossed
jerk boyfriend, when all the signs, be they

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:33.740
supernatural or mundane, have gathered to
tell you that this romance is… uh… kinda

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:34.740
messy.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:39.870
Plus, spooky manors and constantly roaring
winds…

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:48.950
We must be in the Bronte Zone.

00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:54.300
Jane Eyre by Charlotte Bronte was there for
the weird girls, the quiet ones who watched

00:00:54.300 --> 00:01:01.719
and listened, the ones who pined away for
someone to accept them in all of their weird,

00:01:01.719 --> 00:01:04.129
dark glory.

00:01:04.129 --> 00:01:09.640
But in the nearly 175 years since its publication,
the collective definition of what it means

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:15.820
to be “a woman on the outside of society”
has changed and expanded dramatically—and

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:22.400
yet here we are, still dissecting Charlotte
Bronte’s words and gravitating towards Jane

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:23.400
as a
protagonist.

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:29.990
For those of you who have never read Jane
Eyre or enjoyed one of the 8000 films, television,

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:36.430
stage, or radio adaptations not to mention
countless literary retelling here we go.

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:46.650
Written and published in 1847 under the pen
name Currer Bell, Jane Eyre concerns the life

00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:52.590
story of it’s titular heroine, from childhood
up to her mid-20s.

00:01:52.590 --> 00:02:00.600
Jane, an orphaned child left to the care of
her wealthy but highly abusive aunt and cousins,

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:05.630
who verbally, emotionally, and physically
torment her to the point where any self-defense

00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:08.590
is labeled as her acting out.

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:15.560
She is sent away to an austere boarding school
for girls, where the headmaster is equally

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:20.540
abusive and cruel, watching as her fellow
classmates are also punished,near-starved

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:25.730
and, in the case of her best friend there,
left to die from infectious disease.

00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:31.730
Several years later, after reaching adulthood,
Jane leaps at the chance to see more of the

00:02:31.730 --> 00:02:36.910
world when she takes a governess position
at the totally not creepy mansion, Thornfield

00:02:36.910 --> 00:02:41.880
Hall, which is owned by the curmudgeonly Edward
Rochester.

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:46.860
From there on, the story splits into two paths:
the “I’m totally in love with this jerkface

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:52.850
boss who lies to me and plays mind games with
me because I’m the help and completely below

00:02:52.850 --> 00:02:55.390
his station” romance

00:02:55.390 --> 00:03:01.709
And the “wait why do I hear creepy laughing
and footsteps every single night AND WOW WHO

00:03:01.709 --> 00:03:03.780
IS SETTING BEDS ON FIRE?”

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:05.769
Gothic mystery.

00:03:05.769 --> 00:03:15.090
Spoiler alert: It’s Mr. Rochester’s mentally
ill-wife, Bertha.

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:21.550
Also spoiler alert: Jane doesn’t find out
Bertha exists until seconds before she almost

00:03:21.550 --> 00:03:22.720
marries Mr. Rochester.

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:25.400
Real keeper of a guy.

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:26.870
We can fix him.

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:31.500
Will Jane and Mr. Rochester’s dark, torrid
romance finally be realized?

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:36.280
Will Jane find the self-love and confidence
to stand up for herself.

00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:38.310
Will—wait.

00:03:38.310 --> 00:03:42.080
He LOCKED a woman in an attic?

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:44.700
Like, this isn’t a joke??

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:46.800
Jane Austen this isn’t.

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:51.280
While immensely popular and beloved today,
having sold hundreds of thousands of copies

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:56.830
in the last fifty years alone and translated
into dozens of foreign languages (including

00:03:56.830 --> 00:04:03.920
ESPERANTO!), it is perhaps for the best that
Eyre was published under a pseudonym, as critics

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:10.379
were eager to rip it into shreds—for its
comparatively dark, moody heroine and content.

00:04:10.379 --> 00:04:14.989
Said prominent contemporary critic and historian
Elizabeth, Lady Eastlake of the book:

00:04:14.989 --> 00:04:20.789
"We do not hesitate to say that the tone of
mind and thought which has overthrown authority

00:04:20.789 --> 00:04:27.729
and violated every code human and divine abroad,
and fostered Chartism and rebellion at home,

00:04:27.729 --> 00:04:31.159
is the same which has also written Jane Eyre.”

00:04:31.159 --> 00:04:32.159
Damn.

00:04:32.159 --> 00:04:37.689
But where Jane Eyre becomes important is its
place in the tradition of the Bildungsroman—or,

00:04:37.689 --> 00:04:41.110
simply, a novel about a person’s “formative
years”.

00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:46.379
While Jane Eyre is not the first novel told
from a lower- middle class woman’s POV,

00:04:46.379 --> 00:04:51.229
it is the first notable Bildungsroman that
has one as its main subject.

00:04:51.229 --> 00:04:56.039
It is also told in the first person, putting
the reader directly into her thoughts and

00:04:56.039 --> 00:04:58.520
forming world view across the years.

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:04.650
The idea of The Self as a priority in the
text since Jane isn’t guided by heroics,

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:07.840
but what she thinks is good for her and what
will keep her alive.

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:13.069
Towards the end of the book, Jane is presented
with the opportunity to be a good, middle-class

00:05:13.069 --> 00:05:19.789
English citizen when the stuffy, self-righteous
minister St. John proposes to her and promises

00:05:19.789 --> 00:05:23.669
to take her with him to India as a missionary.

00:05:23.669 --> 00:05:24.669
#morecolonization.

00:05:24.669 --> 00:05:31.949
But at the last minute, Jane takes a long
look at him and is like, mmmm, nah, I’m

00:05:31.949 --> 00:05:37.740
gonna go back to the crazy, philandering,
lying bag of hammers that is Mr. Rochester.

00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:44.830
Byronic men do be hitting.

00:05:44.830 --> 00:05:46.819
Says professor of literature Sharon Locy:

00:05:46.819 --> 00:05:52.009
“In this respect, Jane Eyre is atypical
in its appropriation of the male pattern of

00:05:52.009 --> 00:05:54.729
development for its protagonist.

00:05:54.729 --> 00:05:58.699
Jane's story begins, as theirs do, in her
childhood, when she is ten.

00:05:58.699 --> 00:06:04.719
[...] ... Female protagonists of the courtship
novels mentioned above rarely ever leave home,

00:06:04.719 --> 00:06:09.539
or do so only within the prescribed orbits
of young women preparing to marry.

00:06:09.539 --> 00:06:13.210
[…] Jane Eyre, on the other hand, moves
through the world like a boy.”

00:06:13.210 --> 00:06:17.690
But if we are gonna talk about Jane Eyre/Rochester
and what this book has meant for feminist

00:06:17.690 --> 00:06:24.841
literature then we need to talk about Bertha,
Rochester’s wife that he kept in the attic

00:06:24.841 --> 00:06:27.630
for over a decade because … women be crazy.

00:06:27.630 --> 00:06:32.029
Bertha is the “trope namer” of the term
“Madwoman in the Attic.”

00:06:32.029 --> 00:06:37.479
Women in the 19th century were “confined”
to make women either angels or monsters and

00:06:37.479 --> 00:06:40.399
in that binary we have Jane and Bertha.

00:06:40.399 --> 00:06:45.159
In their foundational criticism on Jane Eyre,
The Madwoman in the Attic, authors Sandra

00:06:45.159 --> 00:06:50.959
Gilbert and Susan Gubar define this binary
between the two women as the main confrontation

00:06:50.959 --> 00:06:52.740
of the novel, calling it:

00:06:52.740 --> 00:06:58.979
“a secret dialogue of self and soul on whose
outcome, as we shall see, the novel's plot,

00:06:58.979 --> 00:07:02.639
Rochester’s fate, and Jane's coming of age
all depend.

00:07:02.639 --> 00:07:08.369
[...] Bertha, in other words, is Jane's truest
and darkest double: the angry aspect of the

00:07:08.369 --> 00:07:14.440
orphan child, the ferocious secret self Jane
has been trying to repress ever since her

00:07:14.440 --> 00:07:16.039
days at Gateshead.

00:07:16.039 --> 00:07:17.039
“

00:07:17.039 --> 00:07:21.740
Bertha Mason in Jane Eyre is the evil ghost
like woman who haunts Thornfield Hall,

00:07:21.740 --> 00:07:25.869
only seen through the perspective of Rochester
who views her as a toxic beauty.

00:07:25.869 --> 00:07:27.609
“I thought I loved her.

00:07:27.609 --> 00:07:33.679
… Her relatives encouraged me; competitors
piqued me; she allured me: a marriage was

00:07:33.679 --> 00:07:36.669
achieved almost before I knew where I was.

00:07:36.669 --> 00:07:40.459
Oh, I have no respect for myself when I think
of that act!

00:07:40.459 --> 00:07:45.629
…. I never loved, I never esteemed, I did
not even know her.”

00:07:45.629 --> 00:07:49.899
Alexa, play the world’s smallest violin.

00:07:49.899 --> 00:07:55.449
Rochester sees himself as a victim of the
Mason family who didn’t tell him about her

00:07:55.449 --> 00:08:00.999
mental state and when she started to deteriorate,
Rochester locked her in the house for ten

00:08:00.999 --> 00:08:01.999
years.

00:08:01.999 --> 00:08:05.909
Which, I’m sure 100% had no side effects
on her mental health whatsoever.

00:08:05.909 --> 00:08:09.529
And we are supposed to root for this guy?

00:08:09.529 --> 00:08:15.520
Bertha stands in the way of Jane and Rochester,
because Rochester never “divorced” her

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:19.569
and so he would be a bigamist if he married
Jane.

00:08:19.569 --> 00:08:24.239
While Bertha’s “race” is and can be
assumed to be white there is an implication

00:08:24.239 --> 00:08:30.550
of otherness in how he compares Jane to Bertha
due to Bertha being Creole (a white person

00:08:30.550 --> 00:08:31.989
raised in the West Indies).

00:08:31.989 --> 00:08:39.959
So she got a little too much seasoning for
Rochester’s pallet.

00:08:39.959 --> 00:08:46.149
When relating why he was picked by the Mason’s
Rochester says “Her family wished to secure

00:08:46.149 --> 00:08:49.339
me, because I was of a good race, and so did
she.”

00:08:49.339 --> 00:08:53.889
This could have many implications, including
that Bertha’s family may have, somewhere

00:08:53.889 --> 00:08:59.360
down the line, mixed with the Black population
and were no longer fully white.

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:04.600
As Susan L Meyer, Professor of English at
Wellesley College explains: “An interpretation

00:09:04.600 --> 00:09:09.069
of the significance of the British empire
in Jane Eyre must begin by making sense of

00:09:09.069 --> 00:09:15.279
Bertha Mason Rochester, the mad, drunken West
Indian wife whom Rochester keeps locked up

00:09:15.279 --> 00:09:18.660
on the third floor of his ancestral mansion.”

00:09:18.660 --> 00:09:25.940
Bertha is killed in the burning of Thornfield
Hall, which also blinds Rochester.

00:09:25.940 --> 00:09:29.550
She jumps to her death, freeing herself from
imprisonment.

00:09:29.550 --> 00:09:32.089
Good for her

00:09:32.089 --> 00:09:37.040
How sympathetic Bertha was supposed to be
in the 19th century is hard to understand,

00:09:37.040 --> 00:09:38.949
since she is such a haunting figure.

00:09:38.949 --> 00:09:44.440
But in a post-colonial sense, Bertha is utterly
tragic.

00:09:44.440 --> 00:09:49.000
Especially because Rochester basically says
“it wasn’t me” in terms of taking any

00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:54.319
accountability for the situation that caused
them to be married.

00:09:54.319 --> 00:09:56.770
Grow up dude.

00:09:56.770 --> 00:10:01.589
Hence why we need to go to the greatest way
readers can examine text: fanfiction with

00:10:01.589 --> 00:10:02.690
Wide Sargasso Sea.

00:10:02.690 --> 00:10:08.290
Written by white Dominica author Jean Rhys,
Wide Sargasso Sea is a response to Jane Eyre

00:10:08.290 --> 00:10:09.290
which tells “Bertha’s” story.

00:10:09.290 --> 00:10:16.160
Bertha becomes Antoinette Cosway an heiress
in Jamaica and the story is broken into three

00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:21.319
parts that discuss her childhood, marriage
to a “nameless English man,” and then

00:10:21.319 --> 00:10:24.560
her fate in England as a captive.

00:10:24.560 --> 00:10:28.939
Part One we see Antoinette’s childhood on
a sugar plantation that has gone belly up

00:10:28.939 --> 00:10:32.440
since slavery was abolished (yay, suck it).

00:10:32.440 --> 00:10:38.430
Antoinette’s mother attempts to marry a
wealthy Englishman in order to fix their money

00:10:38.430 --> 00:10:40.100
problems.

00:10:40.100 --> 00:10:46.550
The freed slaves end up burning down the plantation,
which results in Antoinette’s brother passing

00:10:46.550 --> 00:10:48.580
away.

00:10:48.580 --> 00:10:51.610
The mental health struggles end up escalating
for both Antoinette and her surviving family

00:10:51.610 --> 00:10:52.610
members.

00:10:52.610 --> 00:10:56.029
One of the most iconic scenes of the book
is when Antoinette goes to a Black Jamaican

00:10:56.029 --> 00:11:00.769
girl named Tia to try and escape the reality
of her burning home during the small revolt:

00:11:00.769 --> 00:11:05.279
“As I ran, I thought, I will live with Tia
and I will be like her.

00:11:05.279 --> 00:11:10.410
[...]When I was close I saw the jagged stone
in her hand but I did not see her throw it.

00:11:10.410 --> 00:11:15.160
I did not feel it either, only something wet,
running down my face.

00:11:15.160 --> 00:11:19.730
I looked at her and I saw her face crumple
up as she began to cry.

00:11:19.730 --> 00:11:23.450
We stared at each other, blood on my face,
tears on hers.

00:11:23.450 --> 00:11:25.740
It was as if I saw myself.

00:11:25.740 --> 00:11:26.740
Like in a looking-glass.”

00:11:26.740 --> 00:11:31.939
The character of Tia is the foil for Bertha
as a Black girl of the same age.

00:11:31.939 --> 00:11:38.040
During the fire Bertha/Antoinette goes to
Tia and Tia throws a rock at her because despite

00:11:38.040 --> 00:11:43.709
Antoinette thinking they are the same, Tia
knows she is the daughter of freed slaves

00:11:43.709 --> 00:11:44.709
and Antoinette is the daughter of slave owners.

00:11:44.709 --> 00:11:48.810
Just in the same way the English Jane is a
perfect example of white superiority over

00:11:48.810 --> 00:11:51.959
Bertha and her implied otherness.

00:11:51.959 --> 00:11:57.279
Part Two: Rochester complains a lot about
being in the Carribean.

00:11:57.279 --> 00:12:02.199
“I hated the mountains and the hills, the
rivers and the rain.

00:12:02.199 --> 00:12:09.149
I hated the sunsets of whatever colour, I
hated its beauty and its magic and the secret

00:12:09.149 --> 00:12:10.660
I would never know.

00:12:10.660 --> 00:12:16.439
I hated its indifference and the cruelty which
was part of its loveliness.

00:12:16.439 --> 00:12:18.779
Above all I hated her.

00:12:18.779 --> 00:12:21.939
For she belonged to the magic and the loveliness.

00:12:21.939 --> 00:12:28.170
She had left me thirsty and all my life would
be thirst and longing for what I had lost

00:12:28.170 --> 00:12:29.410
before I found it.”

00:12:29.410 --> 00:12:30.730
It’s too hot.

00:12:30.730 --> 00:12:32.939
It’s got too many bugs.

00:12:32.939 --> 00:12:37.250
Man I miss England.

00:12:37.250 --> 00:12:45.089
Also, I don’t respect or care about my wife’s
mental health so I’m gonna mess around and

00:12:45.089 --> 00:12:56.339
rub it in her face, because she may be “crazy”
but I’m a man.

00:12:56.339 --> 00:13:02.940
What we see in Rochester in this passage is
a man who feels unable to deal with things

00:13:02.940 --> 00:13:06.970
he can not control and what he can not control
he loathes.

00:13:06.970 --> 00:13:13.110
A sentiment that doesn’t seem so far off
from the man we meet in Jane Eyre

00:13:13.110 --> 00:13:17.459
Part Three: Antoinette’s transformation
to Bertha is complete.

00:13:17.459 --> 00:13:23.430
She has lost all sense of time, as you would
when you are stuck, in an attic, for ten years.

00:13:23.430 --> 00:13:28.360
“There is no looking glass here and I don't
know what I am like now.

00:13:28.360 --> 00:13:33.070
I remember watching myself brush my hair and
how my eyes looked back at me.

00:13:33.070 --> 00:13:38.279
The girl I saw was myself yet not quite myself.

00:13:38.279 --> 00:13:42.100
Long ago when I was a child and very lonely
I tried to kiss her.

00:13:42.100 --> 00:13:48.319
But the glass was between us—hard, cold
and misted over with my breath.

00:13:48.319 --> 00:13:49.920
Now they have taken everything away.

00:13:49.920 --> 00:13:55.079
What am I doing in this place and who am I?”

00:13:55.079 --> 00:13:57.470
Literary Theorist Gayatri Chakravorty Spivak
(Gai-uh-tree chaa-kruh- vor- tee spi-vuhk)

00:13:57.470 --> 00:14:01.569
explains “In the figure of Antoinette, whom
in Wide Sargasso Sea Rochester violently renames

00:14:01.569 --> 00:14:07.810
Bertha, Rhys suggests that so intimate a thing
as personal and human identity might be determined

00:14:07.810 --> 00:14:09.470
by the politics of imperialism.

00:14:09.470 --> 00:14:15.139
Antoinette, as a white Creole child growing
up at the time of emancipation in Jamaica,

00:14:15.139 --> 00:14:19.410
is caught between the English imperialist
and the black native…

00:14:19.410 --> 00:14:24.350
In this fictive England, she must play out
her role, act out of the transformation of

00:14:24.350 --> 00:14:30.149
her “self” into that fictive Other, set
fire to the house and kill herself, so that

00:14:30.149 --> 00:14:35.749
Jane Eyre can become the feminist individualist
heroine of British fiction.."

00:14:35.749 --> 00:14:42.160
Wide Sargasso Sea is exhibit A about how fan
fiction and metatextual examinations can have

00:14:42.160 --> 00:14:46.079
real literary value … when copyright doesn’t
get in the way

00:14:46.079 --> 00:14:51.899
It doesn’t make Jane Eyre a weaker book,
but highlights things that Bronte would not

00:14:51.899 --> 00:14:53.620
have been aware of.

00:14:53.620 --> 00:14:59.990
Bronte talks about the realities of poor working
women and does great work of giving sad, awkward

00:14:59.990 --> 00:15:04.220
brunettes a true heroine, but then you get
Bertha.

00:15:04.220 --> 00:15:11.529
A mentally ill woman who has been put into
a horrific situation by her husband and the

00:15:11.529 --> 00:15:13.029
book goes:

00:15:13.029 --> 00:15:15.130
Jane what am I?

00:15:15.130 --> 00:15:18.040
Responsible for actually getting to know the
woman I’m going to marry?

00:15:18.040 --> 00:15:19.589
This is the 19th Century.

00:15:19.589 --> 00:15:32.699
She’s lucky I have money and am white.

00:15:32.699 --> 00:15:36.850
In perhaps one of the most famous lines from
the novel, Jane, thinking Mr. Rochester is

00:15:36.850 --> 00:15:42.350
to marry a woman of society, cries out to
her star-crossed lover:

00:15:42.350 --> 00:15:48.910
“Do you think, because I am poor, obscure,
plain, and little, I am soulless and heartless?

00:15:48.910 --> 00:15:54.399
You think wrong!–I have as much soul as
you,–and full as much heart! [...] it is

00:15:54.399 --> 00:15:59.649
my spirit that addresses your spirit; just
as if both had passed through the grave, and

00:15:59.649 --> 00:16:04.499
we stood at God’s feet, equal,–as we are!”

00:16:04.499 --> 00:16:09.470
Jane Eyre the novel’s plea to the humanity
of women, of the lower stationed, is both

00:16:09.470 --> 00:16:13.730
its greatest staying power and one of its
most glaring weaknesses, in how it manages

00:16:13.730 --> 00:16:19.390
to advocate for acceptance while still creating
an Other in Bertha, who is not extended the

00:16:19.390 --> 00:16:21.100
same sort of compassion.

00:16:21.100 --> 00:16:27.230
In many ways Bertha is a foil to Helen Burns,
Jane’s childhood friend who dies young,

00:16:27.230 --> 00:16:28.660
and is the perfect Christian.

00:16:28.660 --> 00:16:32.069
Bertha is all the women that society saw as
impure.

00:16:32.069 --> 00:16:36.249
Impure in their race, impure in their mental
health, and dangerous because of both.

00:16:36.249 --> 00:16:43.059
It is racist and ableist Victorian b.s., but
it has made Bertha a legend and her burning

00:16:43.059 --> 00:16:47.660
Rochester’s house is one of literature’s
best “good for her” moments.

00:16:47.660 --> 00:16:54.119
And all this over a man who literally dresses
in racist drag so he can manipulate two women

00:16:54.119 --> 00:16:57.740
in hopes of making Jane jealous.

00:16:57.740 --> 00:17:03.889
Justice for Bertha and good for Jane, for
waiting until she secured her own bag to get

00:17:03.889 --> 00:17:33.049
her Byronic husband, because sadly viewer,
she married him.

